{"prompt_index":23,"content":"### 4. Make reasoning visible\n\nCreate `docs/DESIGN.md` (or a prominent README section) covering:\n\n- **Problem and scope:** what the brief asks for and what you deliberately left out.\n- **Key decisions and why:** faults as parameter changes in a solver rather than signal multipliers; process truth kept separate from sensor readings; ground truth in its own file; one generator for all scales; sensor placement with paired flows.\n- **Trade-offs:** steady-state hydraulics per minute versus transients; short runs to hit exactly 100,000 rows versus a diurnal cycle; static reserve tanks; synthetic noise assumptions.\n- **Testing approach:** unit tests, golden values, exception fixtures, SQLite join checks, reproducibility hashes.\n- **Limitations:** the ones you already wrote, including that noise values are assumptions, thresholds are provisional, and the alarm consistency check is not an independent benchmark.\n- **How you know it works:** point to specific checks and numbers (true residual about 1e-9, 0.57% false exceedance against a 99% envelope, matching hashes).\n\n###\n\n| Requirement                                                                         | Status                                                                                              |\n| ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Latest commit **pushed** to the public repo                                         | You said committed. Run `git status` and `git log origin/main..HEAD` to confirm nothing is unpushed |\n| Clean-clone run from the README, tests passing without `data/`                      | Not confirmed                                                                                       |\n| Full 100,000-row dataset visible to a reviewer                                      | Decide between committing CSVs, a release asset, or a prominent regenerate note                     |\n| Prompt history in `docs/prompt_history/`                                            | Not mentioned                                                                                       |\n| `docs/AI_USAGE.md` in your own words                                                | Not mentioned                                                                                       |\n| `docs/DESIGN.md` covering decisions, trade-offs, limitations, how you know it works | Not mentioned                                                                                       |\n| `docs/DEVLOG.md` if the commit history is thin                                      | Not mentioned                                                                                       |\n| Walkthrough rehearsal                                                               | Not mentioned                                                                                       |\n\n"}
{"prompt_index":24,"content":"1. **I supplied... selected... reviewed” is vague, and some of it may overstate.** State who proposed what. For instance, if the solver approach, paired sensors and detectability rule came from AI suggestions that you chose to adopt, say that. Claim your own role only where it’s true: you directed scope, ran the checks, decided which suggestions to accept, and so on.\n2. **It lacks the sections reviewers look for.** Add:&#x20;\n   - Where AI accelerated you (code drafting, tests, docs)\n   - Where you relied on it heavily (be candid, probably most code generation)\n   - What **you personally** reviewed, changed or validated, with specifics (for example “checked that the leak flow gap equals the modeled leak”, “ran the reproducibility check”, “pushed back on the mass-balance claim”)\n   - At least one or two concrete cases where AI output was wrong, weak or overstated and you caught it (for example the mass-balance closure being partly by construction, or the 1.0 leak threshold sitting at the sweep’s edge)\n   - What you did **not** verify (for example no real plant data, no clean-clone test yet)\n3. **Check that the first-person text is yours.** It reads like assistant-generated text. That’s acceptable if you’ve read it and it’s accurate, but rewrite it in your own words where it doesn’t match what you did.\n4. 1\\. Push to the repo (do this first)\n\n   Your checkout has no remote. Use the URL you gave me earlier:\n   ```less\n   git remote add origin https://github.com/sarveshraghavan/yottaflex_fde_project.git\n   git branch -M main\n   git push -u origin main\n   ```\n   - If GitHub says the remote has commits you don’t have (for example an auto-created README), run `git pull --rebase origin main` and then push. Don’t force-push without checking what’s on the remote.\n   - GitHub search finding nothing may mean the repo is empty, private, or not created yet. Open the URL in a private window afterwards. If it’s private, change it under Settings > Danger Zone > Change visibility.\n   - After pushing, do the clean-clone check yourself, since the earlier attempt was declined: clone into a new folder, create a virtualenv, run the README commands exactly, and confirm the tests pass without `data/`.\n   - 2\\. DESIGN.md: strong, with two gaps\n\n     It covers what the brief’s fourth requirement asks for: scope, decisions, trade-offs, testing, limits and evidence with specific numbers. The limitations are candid and match your data (the 0.57% exceedance, the near-zero true residual being by construction, the self-referential alarm check).\n     - **“Simulate operating changes” isn’t addressed.** The brief’s one-line objective has two halves, detecting abnormal conditions and simulating operating changes. Your document frames the repo as a dataset generator and fault-signature prototype, but never says whether operating-change simulation is in or out. Either add a small what-if command (the solver already supports it), or list it under “deliberately out of scope” with a reason. Leaving it silent invites a walkthrough question you can’t answer well.\n     - **The tests didn’t grow.** The document says 18 tests, the same number as before lifecycle and event validation were added. Add a few failing-case tests for those validators (invalid transition, broken event chain, missing restoration, bridge mismatch), then update the count. Otherwise a reviewer asking “how is the lifecycle validator tested?” gets “through the full validator run”.\n"}
{"prompt_index":25,"content":"The clean-clone result is good, and 23 tests with failure cases for the lifecycle and bridge validators closes the testing gap. The push is the blocker.\n\n## Why the push failed\n\nA 403 means GitHub recognised the account but denied it write access. The repo URL you gave me belongs to `sarveshraghavan`, and the credentials used were for `r-sarvesh`. That's either a different account, or the repo doesn't exist under that name. Earlier, a GitHub search found no matching repo either, so it may never have been created, or it's private.\n\nNeither I nor the coding assistant can fix this. You need to sign in as the account that owns the repo.\n\n## Fix it from your own terminal\n\n1. **Find out which account owns what.** Log in to github.com as `sarveshraghavan` and check that `yottaflex_fde_project` exists. If it doesn't, create it (empty, public, with no README, license or .gitignore, to avoid a merge conflict on the first push). If `r-sarvesh` is also yours, you can use that account instead and submit its URL.\n2. **Clear the cached credentials.** On Windows, open Credential Manager > Windows Credentials, and remove the `git:https://github.com` entries. Otherwise Git keeps sending the `r-sarvesh` login.\n3. **Authenticate as the right account.** Either run `gh auth login` and pick that account, or use a personal access token (scope `repo`) when Git prompts for a password.\n4. **Push:**&#x20;\n   ```graphql\n   git remote -v                      # confirm the URL matches the repo you owngit push -u origin main\n\n   ```\n   If the remote already has commits, run `git pull --rebase origin main` first. Don't force-push.\n\nKeep tokens out of chats, commits and the README. If one has been pasted anywhere, revoke it in GitHub settings and create a new one.\n\n## Verify after it lands\n\n- Open the repo URL in a private window and confirm it loads without logging in.\n- Check that `docs/`, `sample-data/`, `src/`, the tests and the README are all present, and that `data/` is absent (git-ignored).\n- Clone it into a new folder one more time and run the README's commands. This confirms that what's on GitHub, not just your local copy, works.\n- Check `git log` on GitHub for the commit history, including `9b1c0af`.\n- Submit the exact URL, without the `.git` suffix.\n\n## Last items\n\n- **AI_USAGE.md:** confirm it names every tool you used, including Claude, alongside Codex. Your prompt history for this project includes this conversation, and reviewers will compare the two. If the file credits only Codex, add Claude and say what it contributed (design review, validation suggestions, the checklists).\n- **Prompt history:** add this conversation to `docs/prompt_history/`.\n- **Walkthrough:** rehearse it aloud once. Be ready to explain why `brentq`, where the 25.06 threshold comes from, why the alarm check isn't an independent benchmark, and what you caught or corrected in AI output.\n- **Operating-change simulation:** you've now scoped it out in the design document. Be ready to say why, and what you'd add first (a `what-if` command that re-runs the solver with a changed valve opening).\n\nOnce the push works, tell me what the repo shows, and I can review the README and `AI_USAGE.md` text if you paste them.\n"}
