{"prompt_index":27,"content":"This README is strong on honesty and on commands that reviewers can copy. The limits are stated plainly (true residual closure by construction, assumed noise, alarms not an independent benchmark), and the clean-clone section gets a reviewer to something verifiable in two commands. My points are about scanning speed and portability.\n\n## Fix before you submit\n\n1. **The venv activation is Windows-only.** `.venv\\Scripts\\Activate.ps1` fails on macOS or Linux, and reviewers may use either. Show both:\n   ```shell\n   python -m venv .venv\n   # Windows (PowerShell):  .venv\\Scripts\\Activate.ps1\n   # macOS / Linux:         source .venv/bin/activate\n\n   ```\n   Also say \"use `python3` if `python` isn't found\".\n2. **There is no \"expected results\" section.** The brief says reviewers should be able to \"verify key behavior\", but the README tells them what to run, not what they should see. Add a short table near the top:\n   | Command                                 | Expected                                                             |\n   | --------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n   | `validate_sqlite.py --data sample-data` | zero FK violations, 10,000 telemetry rows, all fixtures flagged      |\n   | `unittest discover`                     | 23 tests pass                                                        |\n   | full generation                         | 100,000 telemetry rows, 33 runs, about 15 s, about 123 MB in `data/` |\n   | `repro_network.py`                      | matching row counts and SHA-256 hashes across 25 outputs             |\n   Copy the exact success lines from your own terminal so the table matches reality.\n3. **The \"Model and data limits\" section is two dense paragraphs.** A reviewer will skim and miss the important numbers. Split it into labelled bullets (solver, tanks, noise, residual tolerance, alarms and detectability, sensor placement), or a small table. Make sure each threshold is named once and kept distinct:\n   - Flow-balance tolerance: two-sided 99% envelope, 25.06 m³/h\n   - Window alarm and `detectable`: 3σ over the run window\n   Right now both appear in the same paragraph, and a reader could think they're the same rule.\n4. **Move the scope boundaries into a short list near the top.** The brief's objective includes \"simulate operating changes\", and your README mentions in passing that they aren't simulated. Put a \"Not implemented\" list near the top, so it reads as a decision rather than an omission: operating-change simulation, diurnal demand, a general detector over clean runs, measured noise calibration, transients.\n5. **Make the doc links real markdown links.** In the version you pasted they are plain text, so make sure the repo's file has relative links like `[Design rationale](docs/DESIGN.md)`, and check that each one resolves on GitHub.\n6. **Move the JSON check out of the one-liner.** The `python -c \"...\"` command is long and easily broken by shell quoting differences. A two-line `src/check_json.py` is more portable, and it can print the \"JSON valid\" line you already expect.\n\n## Small improvements\n\n- Add the repo URL and a one-line summary of what the project is before the reviewer note.\n- Add the sample reproducibility command in full, since \"substitute sample-data and `--telemetry-rows 10000`\" is easy to get wrong.\n- Mention the test count and the typical full-generation runtime.\n- Add a short \"Where to look\" list for reviewers: ground truth in `ground_truth.json`, process truth in `process_truth.csv`, alarms in `alarms.csv`, the data dictionary in `docs/data_dictionary.md`.\n- Add a brief AI disclosure line near the end (with the link to `docs/AI_USAGE.md`), so a reviewer reading only the README sees that it exists.\n- Optionally, add a GitHub Actions badge once CI runs the sample validation and tests.\n\n## What I checked in the numbers\n\nThe run counts are consistent: 11 runs per facility (2 clean, 3 boundary, 6 fault) gives 33 runs, 15 fault-free runs and 18 fault runs across 3 facilities. The valve and pump totals (7/7/6 pumps, 20 valves per facility) match the brief.\n\nOnce you've pushed and the repo is public, a final check helps: open the README on GitHub, since it renders differently from your editor, and make sure the tables and links work.\n"}
